<!-- GH-1034, P3 call guards, option persistcalls. churn reads, then
     writes, each of 256 writable globals, with a call after every write and
     one more inside a branch every eighth step: 288 calls. small touches two
     globals across three calls. Pass 1 is upstream placement (option off):
     every global is guarded at every call, and the guards survive dead-code
     removal, so churn's live IR holds 84250 ops. Pass 2 is the default:
     churn's pass guards 319 RAM ranges at 288 calls (91,872 guards), past the
     65,536-guard threshold, so each global keeps only the guards next to its
     accesses (the call before its read, the call after its write, the last
     call before a return or before a join that needs the value after it),
     1053 of its 73,728, and the live IR drops to 3670 ops; the skipped
     guards and joins keep their op numbers, so every pass creates 95006. small, far below the threshold,
     keeps every guard. Pass 3 is persistcalls on: small loses one guard that
     only hands a value to the next (16 ops to 15). Each function prints the
     same body in every pass. Two
     more passes count churn's ops only: at persistcalls 91873, one guard more
     than churn's pass, it keeps them; at 91872 it drops them. The fixture's
     source, provenance and native oracle are beside it. The second fixture
     holds two functions whose C the sparse guards once changed, decompiled
     with the option off and on: revisit reads u0.s[0] on both sides of a call
     before a later pass widens u0 to four bytes (it printed return 1), and
     loopstore stores a value computed before a loop of calls after the loop
     (the store moved in front of the loop). snip and snipsys join sg after
     two calls, then call f2 and f1 (with a system call between them in
     snipsys) on one branch: merging copies the joined value back to sg in
     front of that branch's first guard, so a guard reading the value from
     before an unguarded f2 printed the copy after f2, undoing it.
-->
